  {
    "episode_id": 91236,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91236.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 151,
    "turn_text": "Well, thanks for asking that question, man. Look, at the end of the day, we have to make a fundamental decision in this country. Number one is health care, a human right, or is it not? If it is a human right, then we guarantee health care to every man, woman, and child, regardless of income. And what we say is, if you're sick, if you need to check up, you go to any doctor you want to, you go to any hospital you want to, And you don't have to take out your credit card. You don't have to pay a nickel out of your pocket for that visit. Because that's what health care is if we talk about it as a human right now. That's what exists in Canada. That's what exists in most industrialized countries around the world. And then the second point is we have to ask ourselves, which my opponents are not, is why is it that we are spending twice as much per person on health care as to the people of any other country? That's a profound statement. I got all these conservatives here. They want to save money. We're spending $11,000 a year for personal and health care twice with the Canadian spend, with the French spend, with the German spend. They manage to cover all the people. And the answer is pretty obvious. It gets to the whole heart and soul of this debate. Is the function of health care to make $100 billion in profit for the health care industry, which is what they made last year? Is it to make billions of dollars in profits for the drug companies, medical equipment supplies, or as the function of healthcare to provide healthcare to all people in a cost effect.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Health care is a human right.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "If health care is a human right, then everyone should receive it regardless of income.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "When someone is sick or needs a check-up, they should be able to go to any doctor or hospital without paying anything out of pocket.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The United States spends twice as much per person on health care compared to Canada, France, and Germany.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "In Canada, health care is provided without out-of-pocket costs for patients.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Most industrialized countries provide health care without requiring direct patient payments.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The U.S. spends approximately $11,000 per person annually on health care.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Other countries like Canada, France, and Germany cover all their citizens' health care despite lower spending.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The health care industry in the U.S. made $100 billion in profit last year.",
